Character-defining elements of the Davis Residence include, but are not limited to: 
- Wood-frame construction on a sandstone foundation; narrow-width lapped, drop wooden siding, corner boards and patterned (scalloped and diamond shaped) wood-shingle cladding in gables and balustrade; 
- Hipped roof with lower cross gables, hipped-roof dormer; closed and flat roof deck; wooden tongue-and-groove soffits; buff-brick corbelled exterior chimney;
- Two-storey, rectangular side-hall plan; bay and cut-away bay window protrusions; rear extension;
- Fenestration, with, one-over-one wooden-sash windows; two and four pane wooden storm sashes; some leaded and stain-glass decorative transom lights;
- Open, full-width front verandah with classical-style columns;
- Open second-storey porch with balcony with classical style columns and a shingle-clad (scallop-shaped shingles) balustrade;
- Off-set single-glazed, wood-panelled front door with leaded-glass transom, and wood paneled balcony door.

Interior character-defining elements:
- Original fir floors throughout;
- Open staircase with fir balustrade, newel posts and turned balusters; narrow secondary staircase at the rear;
- Original first-storey fireplace with carved wooden surround and mantel and a decorative brass insert; 
- Paneled, wood pocket-doors;
- second-floor interior door frames with frosted decorative transom lights. 
Contextual character-defining elements:
- original placement of the house on the property;
- soft front-yard landscaping character.

The character-defining elements of the Edmonds Block include, but are not limited to its: 
-	two-storey, rectangular, flat roof form three bays in width with a short façade;  interior light and air shafts;
-	semi-milled construction brick exterior walls; 
-	wraparound buff-coloured brick façade with deeply recessed mortar joints; façade brickwork incorporating soldiered and patterned brickwork;
-	sandstone façade detailing including carved roofline and smooth storefront  cornices, stringcourse, carved doorway hood, pier capitals and base (foundation);
-	fenestration, with symmetrical façade; Neo-classical-style main entrance fanlight; some extant 1-over-1 wooden sash windowsp;
-	metal fire-escape stairway located at the rear of the building;
-	interior foyer with marble wainscot and dado, ornamental plaster cornice, and interior fanlight.

Character-defining elements include, but are not limited to the building¿s:

-three-storey, U-shape plan;
- wood-frame construction with red brick cladding; rough-faced, rusticated sandstone foundation and belt course; smooth sandstone main doorway surround, sills, quoins, pilasters, keystones, and decorative inlays and plaques on front façade with 'YWCA' and '1910' lettering; 
-three-story wood veranda frontispiece with classical corner columns and pedestals and arched lower openings; 
-hipped roof with metal-tile roofing, pressed-metal block modillion roofline cornice and pediment; 
- secondary, pressed metal cornice atop first storey
-wooden-sash, double-hung windows with nine-over-one glazing on first story and twelve-over-one glazing on second and third stories of both north and west elevations; and double-hung windows with single-pane glazing, and basement level casement windows on south elevation;  
-main central entrance with wood doorway assembly with beveled and leaded glass fanlight and sidelights; second and third-storey wood (veranda) doorway assemblies with transom lights and sidelights;
-metal fire escapes on west and south elevations; 
-interior details including panelled wood doors and casings; canvas wainscot detail and baseboards; cast iron radiators; dumb-waiter; fireplace on main floor; and open central staircase with classical balustrades and strapwork ceiling;
-interior layout including central stairway winding up to 2nd and 3rd floor hallways; and
-setback from avenue.

Key elements of  the heritage character include but are not limited to:
- setting on a two block property on the south side of 9th Ave and the north side of 10th Ave between 1st St SE and 1st St SW in the southeastern core of downtown Calgary;
- siting of complex around and including Calgary Tower; and
- stepped concrete plant boxes and artistic pre-cast concrete panel on Palliser Square West wall with coiling detailing; small enclosed park to east of One Palliser Square.

Husky Service Centre
- one-storey with flat angled overhanging roofline; glass curtain wall on front façade; and
- Thunderstone cladding on front façade.

Mount Royal House
- rectangular plan, 24-storey residential tower set on top of an open parking podium with commercial on the ground floor; vertical projections on middle of south and north facades; 
- construction including: structural steel I-beam frame with central concrete core; concrete elements including pre-cast brick panels, panel horizontal banding, vertical beam cage over parking garage, corrugated panels and white stone aggregate panels at main entryway on south facade; closed wooden balconies; 
- Modern design such as: repetition of minimalist detailing with banding, fenestration, and balconies; vertical detailing in concrete banding; scalloped detailing in concrete panels at base of parking garage;
- fenestration including: regular patterned metal-sash sliding windows; and
- exterior details including: curved brackets at base of tower, mimicking brackets visible on Palliser Hotel.

One Palliser Square
- rectangular plan, 26-storey tower with mechanical box at top floor; recessed glass curtain wall entryway; 
- construction including: structural steel I-beam frame with central concrete core; glass curtain wall; pre-cast white stone aggregate concrete panels; 
- Formalist design such as its: patterned panels composed of monolithic concrete on a repetitive grid; fluted corners;
- fenestration including: vertical curtain-wall components consisting of aluminum-sash fixed windows with reflective glass; fully glazed two-storey rounded rectangular plate glass lobby windows with anodized bronze sash and band detail; revolving doors at main entryway; 
- exterior details including: entire first floor with louvre detailing; bronze spandrels interspersed with fixed windows at the building corners; mirrored soffits in entryway; and
- interior features such as: polished marble wall tiles; original elevators and mezzanine.

Palliser Square East and West
- irregular plan, four-storey buildings curved at second storey and progressively step back from the main floor at each storey; enclosed curved stairwell to east of Calgary Tower; +15 access to Palliser Hotel on west façade; 
- construction including: pre-cast white stone aggregate panels and brick panel spandrels over windows; Thunderstone cladding at entryways; 
- Modern design such as its: repetition of minimalist detailing with spandrels, arches, and fenestration;
- fenestration including: aluminum-sash fixed windows with reflective glass at main storey; glass curtain wall with aluminum-sash windows at second storey; reverse arched windows on upper storeys with brick and aggregate pre-cast panels separating tiers of windows; aluminum-sash doors with large rectangular fixed glass windows above; and
- exterior details including: large arched window openings at ground level with projecting signage boxes separating storefront windows from arched windows above.

Palliser East and West Parkades and Central Heating and Cooling Plant
- open and twinned coiled parking structures; open parking lots; Plant encased in centre of Palliser West Parkade; 
- construction including: poured-in-place concrete coiled structures; pre-cast concrete panels; decorative breezeway block surrounding top of Plant; and
- Modern design such as: rectangular vertical and horizontal detailing; pre-cast concrete cage on exterior of Palliser East Parkade.

Key elements that define the heritage character of Northern Electric Co. Warehouse include its:
- form, scale and massing as expressed by its four-storey, rectangular plan with narrow façade; 
- flat roof with brick parapets with moulded frieze (south and west façades); flagstaff; wide brick chimney;
- reinforced concrete construction; concrete structural frame expressed in façade with concrete walls and heavy, rusticated piers on two lower storeys and smooth-finished concrete piers on two upper storeys; original red-brown brick cladding in common bond on east common wall, and stretcher bond on two upper storeys of south and west  walls and three upper storeys of north (rear) wall; 
- original fenestration pattern on all façades; tall vertical windows with 4-light configuration; deeply recessed second-storey windows; moulded panels between the lower storey windows; smooth-finished concrete sills; single-leaf and two-leaf bronze doors with glazed and wooden panels; tile dado; wooden door surround and transom;
- additional exterior elements typical of warehouse with offices: large openings for original full-length display windows; moulded concrete lower signband-cornice on south and west façades; visible arches of original open loading platform;
- front entry portico with ornate opening and granite steps leading to hexagonal-tiled landings; 
- interior features including four-storey staircase with decorative cast and wrought-iron railings and cast-iron newel posts, wooden handrails, tile dados on lower floors, and tile staircase (below protective cover); main entry vestibule with tile dados; plaster ceiling mouldings and hexagonal floor tiles;  
- placement and orientation on deep corner lot; placement on front property line without setback;
- location fronting 11th Avenue, a busy downtown commercial street; and 
- situation just south of the CPR railway right of way in Calgary's historical warehouse district.

Character-defining elements of the property include, but are not limited to, the following:
-	Two-storey, rectangular, flat-roof form with asymmetrical facade; raised basement and ground floor;
-	Heavy-timber (mill) construction with brick exterior; red, pressed-brick facade with heavily corbelled cornice; battered rock-faced sandstone foundation rock-faced sandstone sill course, window sills and lintels;
-	Fenestration, with facade comprising segmental-arched first-storey windows and flat-headed second storey windows; segmental arched rear windows; 
-	Off-centre main entrance doorway and transom light opening;
-	Open porte-cochere with metal cornice and wood tongue-and-groove ceiling containing a recessed secondary front entrance doorway opening of double width with transom light;
-	Three large loading doors at rear leading to an interior, open, one-storey loading area;
-	Commercial, warehouse context adjacent to rail line; building constructed to front, rear, and side lot lines.

The character-defining elements of the Customs House include, but are not limited to its:
-Symmetrically balanced, rectangular, four-storey, flat-roof form;
-Steel-frame and concrete construction with pressed, red-brick cladding (upper storeys) and rock-faced, Glenbow sandstone cladding laid in coursed ashlar (first-storey);
-Brick detailing consisting of pilasters;
-Sandstone detailing consisting of pilaster capitals and bases, window lintels and sills, continuous window hoods with keystones and the front doorway surround;
-Substantial, block-modillion, sheet metal cornice;
-Horizontally and vertically aligned symmetrical fenestration with rectangular and segmental-arched openings; multi-pane, wooden sash window above main entrance;
-Western bay consisting of unenclosed opening, and the loading docks contained within;
-Rooftop flag pole;
-Interior configuration of the split-level lobby/stair hall and main stair well;
-Interior detailing of the lobby/stair hall and main stair well consisting of marble paneling and baseboards and terrazzo flooring; and
-Interior main staircase with iron framework, balustrades, newels and wood hand rails; marble treads.
